  "slug": "maximum-value-of-k-coins-from-piles",
  "title": "Maximum Value of K Coins From Piles",
  "category": "dsa",
  "subcategory": "dynamic-programming",
  "difficulty": "Hard",
  "tags": [
    "Array",
    "Dynamic Programming",
    "Prefix Sum"
  ],
  "statement": "There are `n` **piles** of coins on a table. Each pile consists of a **positive number** of coins of assorted denominations.\n\nIn one move, you can choose any coin on **top** of any pile, remove it, and add it to your wallet.\n\nGiven a list `piles`, where `piles[i]` is a list of integers denoting the composition of the `i^(th)` pile from **top to bottom**, and a positive integer `k`, return _the **maximum total value** of coins you can have in your wallet if you choose **exactly**_ `k` _coins optimally_.\n\n**Example 1:**\n\n![](./assets/maximum-value-of-k-coins-from-piles-1.png)\n```\nInput: piles = [[1,100,3],[7,8,9]], k = 2\nOutput: 101\nExplanation:\nThe above diagram shows the different ways we can choose k coins.\nThe maximum total we can obtain is 101.\n```\n\n**Example 2:**\n\n```\nInput: piles = [[100],[100],[100],[100],[100],[100],[1,1,1,1,1,1,700]], k = 7\nOutput: 706\nExplanation:\nThe maximum total can be obtained if we choose all coins from the last pile.\n```\n\n**Constraints:**\n\n*   `n == piles.length`\n*   `1 <= n <= 1000`\n*   `1 <= piles[i][j] <= 10^(5)`\n*   `1 <= k <= sum(piles[i].length) <= 2000`",
  "constraints": [
    "n == piles.length",
    "1 <= n <= 1000",
    "1 <= piles[i][j] <= 105",
    "1 <= k <= sum(piles[i].length) <= 2000"
  ],
